PART C  rotor spin-DOWN with no active braking: J_r omega' = -k_m omega^2
==============================================================================
  Fixed-pitch unidirectional rotors can only reduce thrust by letting the rotor slow
  against its own aerodynamic drag torque (unless the ESC brakes actively).  The exact
  solution is omega(t) = omega0 / (1 + t/t_c) with t_c = J_r/(k_m omega0), so the
  INITIAL time constant of the decay is t_c and it is NOT symmetric with spin-up.
  hover rotor speed omega_h = 1103.5 rad/s; k_m = 2.370e-08 N m s^2/rad^2 (Sun et al. TABLE II, via the tool)
  PASS spin-down closed form vs numerical integration [rad/s]: got 1023.22, want 1023.22 (tol 0.002)
  ASSUMED rotor+bell polar inertia J_r (not retrieved in the dossier; 5-inch racing
  props with the motor bell are of order 1e-5 kg m^2):
   J_r = 3.0e-06 kg m^2 -> free spin-down time constant t_c =  114.7 ms; time to shed 10 % of thrust   6.2 ms
   J_r = 6.0e-06 kg m^2 -> free spin-down time constant t_c =  229.4 ms; time to shed 10 % of thrust  12.4 ms
   J_r = 1.0e-05 kg m^2 -> free spin-down time constant t_c =  382.4 ms; time to shed 10 % of thrust  20.7 ms
   J_r = 2.0e-05 kg m^2 -> free spin-down time constant t_c =  764.7 ms; time to shed 10 % of thrust  41.4 ms
   J_r = 3.0e-05 kg m^2 -> free spin-down time constant t_c = 1147.1 ms; time to shed 10 % of thrust  62.0 ms
  the J_r that makes t_c equal the MEASURED 39.1 ms thrust time constant (A-03): 1.02e-06 kg m^2
  the J_r that makes t_c equal the measured 33 ms rotor-speed constant (A-04): 8.63e-07 kg m^2
  Reading: a 1e-5 kg m^2 rotor group gives a free-decay constant of the same tens-of-ms
  order as the measured constant, so the measured 'motor time constant' on a
  unidirectional fixed-pitch rotor is largely the rotor's own inertia divided by its
  drag torque, not an electrical limit.  Moving it needs a lighter/smaller rotor, an
  actively braking ESC, or more rotors (same thrust, smaller J_r each).
   half the rotor inertia (3-inch prop class): motor_tau  19.6 ms -> tau_rot   84.3 ms -> v_stop(s=6 m, tau_sense=40 ms, half rotation) 19.70 m/s
